| `02-custom-scan-interval` | Tighter poll interval (local only) | Never (intentional) |
| `03-partial-response-guard` | Partial overview responses no longer crash entity listeners (merged in core `dev`, expected in 2026.12.0) | When core `master` includes the fix (the sync opens an issue) |

## Patches that land upstream

`update.sh` checks each patch with `git apply --check`. When a patch does not apply but `git apply --reverse --check` passes, upstream already contains exactly that change: the script skips the patch, logs a warning and sets `VERISURE_UPSTREAMED_PATCHES` in CI. The workflow then opens one issue per such patch (`Patch NN-name is in upstream, remove it`), unless an open issue with that title exists. The sync continues as normal.

A patch that applies neither forward nor in reverse still fails the sync: upstream changed the same code differently and the patch needs manual work.

## Upstream readiness gate

`update.sh` clones **`home-assistant/core`** branch **`master`** and **skips the sync** (exit 0) until upstream includes:

- `vsure` pinned to at least **2.7.1** in `manifest.json`
- Session-refresh / rate-limit behavior in `coordinator.py` (`COOKIE_REFRESH_INTERVAL`, `_raise_rate_limited`)

While the gate fails, `custom_components/verisure` and `upstream_snapshot/` are left unchanged. CI sets `VERISURE_SYNC_SKIPPED=true` and skips commit/tag/release steps.

Temporary upstream-preview patches (session resilience, broadband guard, vsure pin) are **not** tracked here; the fork ships the last synced component until `master` catches up (expected **2026.7.0**).

## After changing `patches/*.patch`

1. Run `./scripts/update.sh` locally when you can (`jq`, `git`, network clone) — it regenerates patches and verifies they apply. Optional: run `./scripts/regenerate_patch.sh` alone to inspect regenerated files only.
2. If `update.sh` skips (unchanged upstream snapshot), delete `upstream_snapshot/` and rerun to force a full refresh.

## Scripts

- `update.sh` requires **`jq`** and clones **`home-assistant/core`** (branch `master`, shallow).
